The Neon tileset's source
=========================

Neon signs at 32x32, drawn by an AI image generator to a brief written for
this game, sixteen to a sheet, as white tubes on black.  The brief, every
sheet it returned and the reasons each picture was accepted or sent back are
in archive/design_brief_tileset_neon/, which is not in the repository.  They
replaced the set's first pictures, 109 silhouettes typed by hand at 16x16, in
November 2026; the palette and the idea are the same.

    palette.txt     the ten hues and four tones, the colour-blind ones, and
                    which hue and tone each of the game's colours is
    source.png      every accepted picture, cut to 32x32: black, four greys
                    and seven fixed colours.  This is the art.
    source.txt      which picture is where in source.png
    recipe.txt      where each picture is in the brief and how it was cut:
                    which sheet and cell, and any cut or lift of its own
    manifest.txt    which picture each terrain feature, object, trap, spell
                    effect and the player is drawn with
    monsters.txt    which picture each monster is drawn with

`build-neon.py` reads these and writes lib/tiles/neon/32x32.png,
32x32-cb.png and graf-neo.prf: graphics modes 7 and 8 in lib/tiles/list.txt.
`build-neon.py --cut` rewrites source.png and source.txt from the brief's
sheets, and needs the brief and ImageMagick.  `--preview` draws a mock
dungeon and town into this directory, at 3x and at actual size, in both
sheets' colours; those are not committed.

The greys are the thing's colour
--------------------------------

A tile's colour is data.  The greys (#333333 to #FFFFFF, darkest first) take
the thing's own colour from lib/gamedata, through the hues and tones in
palette.txt, keeping the shading: the second grey from the top becomes the
colour itself, the top one a tone paler and the two below it a tone and two
tones darker.  One young dragon is every young dragon, each the colour
monster.txt gives it.  The fixed colours (red, orange, yellow, green, cyan,
blue, magenta, each at four tones) are what the tint does not touch: a
torch's flame, a crown, a jewel.  A fixed colour in the thing's own hue
disappears into the tint, so `--check` lists the monsters that have one.

What the build adds
-------------------

  * Eighteen pictures drawn in code: the floor, grass and dirt (the 16x16
    set's grit, doubled), granite, permanent rock, magma and quartz with and
    without treasure, the mountains, sand, mud, the road, the four bolts and
    the burst.
  * A lift for monsters drawn too dim: under 30% of the lit pixels in the two
    top greys or a colour's pale or neon tone, and every grey goes up a
    level, unless recipe.txt says otherwise.
  * Water, deep water, lava and the world's edge quieted, a grey level or
    two down, so a lake does not outshine what stands in it.
  * Each terrain feature in the game's four lights: as coloured when seen
    or remembered, a tone brighter in torchlight and a tone darker in the
    dark, fixed colours and all.  The plain ground (floor, walls, grass,
    sand, the road) sits at the dim tone, where the 16x16 set put it, so it
    stays behind the figures; any other feature whose colour is a pale one
    sits a tone down, so a torch has somewhere brighter to take it.
  * Rubble, stairs and the town's buildings laid on their floor or grass, and
    each building's number or letter stamped on it.
  * The heroine for a character whose $SEX is Female.

The shimmer
-----------

The sheet is laid out the way the engine's colour cycling expects
(`cycle:10:4:9` in list.txt): blocks of forty rows, ten hues by four tones.
A picture used by a monster that shimmers is stored down one column in all
ten hues, and a treasure that glints in all four tones of its hue, so moving
up and down the column changes the colour and never the picture.  Everything
else is stored once, wherever there is room.  The game only animates them
with the option "Color: Shimmer multi-colored things" (animate_flicker) on.

The colour-blind sheet
----------------------

Mode 8 is the same sheet in palette.txt's cbhue colours, served by the same
prf.  neon-colourblind.py found those colours and says what they save;
palette.txt gives its numbers.

What --check checks
-------------------

  * Every picture in source.png and every one drawn in code is used.
  * Every shimmering monster and glinting treasure, walked through its cycle
    the way src/grafmode.c will, stays on its own picture at every step.
  * The colour-blind sheet: every colour a thing sits on clears 3:1 against
    black for a figure and 2.5:1 for lit terrain; the colours the game gives
    a meaning to (hit points green to yellow to red, a monster's health)
    stay at least 12 CIEDE2000 apart under protanopia, deuteranopia and
    tritanopia; and it is the same picture as the main sheet, pixel for
    pixel, with only the colours changed.

It fails on any of those.  It regenerates the sheets and the prf first, so CI
diffs graf-neo.prf after it.

To change a picture, change its line in recipe.txt and run
`build-neon.py --cut`.  To change which picture something uses, edit
manifest.txt or monsters.txt and run `build-neon.py`.  Check with
`build-neon.py --check` and `audit-tiles.py`.
